// Research reuse rate — is the supplier research ledger actually saving work?
//
// The ledger exists so a reassessment does not re-ask the questions the last
// assessment already answered. That claim is measurable: every lookup the
// delivery Function serves is audited, and a lookup that came back FRESH is a
// web call, a wait and a token spend that did not happen.
//
// Read it as two numbers, not one:
//   * reuse_rate  — of the questions asked, how many were already known and
//                   still fresh. It should climb over a supplier's life and
//                   sit highest on reassessments.
//   * stale_rate  — known, but too old to reuse. A high stale rate is not a
//                   failure: it is the ledger correctly refusing to serve an
//                   answer past its freshness window. A stale rate near 100 %
//                   on a fast-moving source (cisa-kev, first-epss) is normal;
//                   on eur-lex or ISO it means the TTL table is too tight.
//
// A FALLING reuse rate with steady volume usually means one of three things,
// in this order of likelihood: the agents stopped calling lookupResearchLedger
// before searching (charter or overlay drift), the questions are being phrased
// differently each run so the record key never matches, or the ledger list is
// unavailable and every lookup is silently degrading to a miss — which the
// `unavailable` column separates out, because that one is an incident and the
// other two are drift.
//
// Review: monthly with the token-economy loop (operations/TOKEN_ECONOMY_PLAYBOOK.md);
// the ledger's own design is in sharepoint/README.md.
// Target: reuse_rate >= 0.35 across a 30-day window once a supplier has been
// assessed twice; `unavailable` = 0.
// Controls: ISO 27001:2022 A.8.6 (capacity management), A.5.22 (monitoring of
// supplier services — the ledger is how that monitoring stays affordable),
// DORA Art. 9(2) (efficient use of resources).
//
let window = 30d;
AppTraces
| where TimeGenerated > ago(window)
| extend audit = parse_json(tostring(Message))
| where tostring(audit.audit) startswith "research_ledger"
| extend kind = tostring(audit.audit),
         supplier = tostring(audit.supplier),
         sourceId = tostring(audit.sourceId),
         outcome = tostring(audit.outcome)      // fresh | stale | miss | unavailable
| summarize
    asked        = count(),
    reused       = countif(outcome == "fresh"),
    stale        = countif(outcome == "stale"),
    never_seen   = countif(outcome == "miss"),
    unavailable  = countif(outcome == "unavailable")
  by sourceId
| extend reuse_rate = round(1.0 * reused / asked, 3),
         stale_rate = round(1.0 * stale  / asked, 3)
| order by asked desc
